35. Security and technical requirements
Enforce role-based access control.

Separate student and staff borrowing policies.

Prevent duplicate active loans for the same physical copy.

Use database transactions when issuing and returning books.

Prevent simultaneous reservations from conflicting.

Validate file uploads and scan uploaded files for malware.

Store digital resources securely.

Apply copyright and licensing restrictions.

Use signed or authorized resource access rather than public storage URLs.

Record fine adjustments and waivers.

Maintain immutable transaction history for completed loans.

Preserve deleted or archived catalogue history according to retention policy.

Protect student reading history from unnecessary disclosure.

Back up catalogue and digital resource metadata.

Test all borrowing and return workflows.

36. Acceptance criteria
The module is ready for production review when:

Librarians can register books and individual copies.

Each physical copy has a unique barcode.

Students and staff can search the catalogue.

The system can issue and receive books.

Loan periods and limits are configurable.

Renewals and reservations work correctly.

Overdue books are identified automatically.

Fines integrate with Finance.

Lost and damaged books are tracked.

Digital resources have controlled access.

Lecturers can submit course reading lists.

Research submissions can be reviewed and published.

Library attendance is recorded.

Reports are generated with permission checks.

Student and employee identities are reused.

Every sensitive action is audited.

Mobile and desktop interfaces are usable.

Automated tests cover concurrent borrowing, returns, renewals and reservations.

